<p>** IDBS refer to India Development Bonds</p>
<p>Though the cash flows for February show a deficit of over 40 billion rupees, this figure needs to be seen along with the surplus liquidity already existing in the system.</p>
<p>The bid amount of 67 billion rupees at the repo auction and 18 billion rupees at the 364 day treasury auction of which 35 billion and 6.35 billion rupees respectively were accepted, implies that 43.5 billion rupees of short term surpluses are lying univested.</p>
<p>Also any pressure on call money rates will be squared off by lower amount of subscriptions at RBI repo auctions. Hence soft call money rates and easy liquidity conditions should continue till around mid February.</p>
<p>The Reserve Bank is expected to conduct its open market operations very actively over the next fortnight. Four securities have been put up on RBI's sale list: 0% 2000 (I) @ 13.04%, 11.75% 2001 @ 13.30%, 13.82% 2002 @ 13.40% and 14.00% 2005 @ 13.54%.</p>
<p>As RBI gradually squeezes out surplus funds from the market, the one shot impact of IDB redemption and a possible government bond issue is likely to put pressure on the money markets towards the end of next fortnight.</p>
